Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.510 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i had one of the happiest childhoods  until i was seven that's when my father  contracted cerebral malaria  a form of malarial infection that  affects the brain  after having a high fever for about a  week  he developed depression all this while  our family was staying in a really small  town in india  and we had no idea what was going on  with my father  yes we did not understand depression  then  and how it was going to change our lives  today i want to share with you what i  have learned about depression  through my father's journey and my work  in studies  my father seemingly recovered he looked  hail and healthy  but on most days he had a tough time  getting out of bed  and going to work my kind  and loving father stopped smiling  entirely  daily chores including bathing and  eating  became a struggle our family that had  exactly one bread winner  spiraled into financial troubles  but what i remember feeling then is not  so much the financial impact  but the debilitating emotional impact it  had on our family  people around us although  well-intentioned  made things worse family and friends  visited home and gave my mother  unsolicited advice  take him to this priest get him a ring  with that gemstone  many even said that my father looked  just fine and might be  faking being unwell that  enraged me a then teenager  i spent my teenage years grappling to  understand what was going on with my  father  and why his personality had completely  changed  i wanted to do everything everything a  teenager could  to make things better  i started with making cutouts of  newspaper articles on mental health  and saving them neatly into my fire  article by article my interest grew  eventually inspired by my father's  journey i got a phd in neuroscience  i spent years in a lab doing experiments  to understand the origins of anxiety and  depression  later i worked with many organizations  to make people aware of depression's  existence  going by who projections one in four  individuals listening to this talk are  going to be impacted by at least  one episode of depression in their  lifetime  depression is everywhere and depression  is  not a choice what might be the reasons  behind depression  scientists report it could be genetic  vulnerability  or environmental stressors or a  combination of both  having a financial distress a chronic  illness due to another disease  sexual dysfunction obesity a past  traumatic life incident  triggers for depression can go on and on  depression is correlated with having a  variety of chemical imbalances  in our brains about 70 percent  of patients treated for depression start  showing improvement in their symptoms  within four to six weeks of starting  treatment  this number stands to attest that  depression is treatable  but unlike the patients of cardiac  disorders  60 to 80 percent patients of depression  never receive proper treatment why  foremost because of the stigma our  society tags  with having a mental health disorder  people with depression  they are not seen in the same light as  people with having a cardiac disorder  as society we have accepted that a heart  attack could be fatal  so we see quickest medical support to  address it  unfortunately we do not look at  depression in the same manner  untreated depression is one of the  leading causes  of suicides across the world and yet  we do not encourage quickest treatment  or indeed any treatment for it  we can only create change if we make  people  feel comfortable if we let them say  yes i need support  my father had a rare process of  developing depression through cerebral  malaria  he had to be on a lifelong medication  for most individuals depression is not  instant there is often a long trajectory  to it  and in that window lies your power  to support that individual  so when do you know that the person  needs support  what can you do to help the person i'm  going to share a three-step  approach first understand the symptoms  of depression  depression is a health condition it is  not a personal choice  it does include many symptoms as my  father's illness did  loss of appetite or overeating  difficulty sleeping or sleeping a lot  feelings of hopelessness helplessness  and sadness  lack of energy and fatigue  loss of desire to engage in activities  that the person previously  enjoyed and last  having suicidal thoughts  look for these symptoms  step two offer support and encouragement  knowing that it could be a long journey  denying the problem is not going to make  it go away  your loved one is not lazy or  unmotivated  when a person is going through  depression telling them to get on with  life  and just cheer up is not going to take  the problem away  have patience and kindness towards your  loved one  as you encourage them to seek help from  mental health care professionals  recognize that taking care of a person  going through depression  is not an easy task so be gentle on  yourself  as much as you may want you cannot  rescue them from their depression  you cannot fix the problem for them  step three do what you can as a next  generation leader  if you're in a place where you can  change the way workplaces support mental  health  i encourage you to take action towards  it  estimates suggest that major depression  alone has been costing  employees 50 billion dollars per year in  lost productivity  now these numbers are projected when we  did not see current pandemic coming at  us  under the visible pandemic there is an  epidemic of depression that has been  flaring up  i will continue to push boundaries in  the way mental health care is supported  and perceived i'm not asking you to  devote your lifetime towards it  as i have but i do want to  invite you to join me in normalizing the  conversation around mental health  join me in saving those lives before  it's too late  stand up for a quarter of world's  population here  and in case you're wondering about my  father this is my father from last year  when he visited me  he was and continues to be a wonderful  person  and an inspiration for me he leads a  happy  healthy life with the help of medication  if it happened to you or a loved one  i want you to know it does not have to  be a permanent state  depression is treatable provided we get  access to the right resources

